cellFont: render surface, horizontal glyph images, font instances, OnMemory fontsets; SDK argument order for the open functions.
Toro's own patch (7xxx).
What: implements the five cellFont imports Toro calls that had no implementation and fell to the unresolved-NID
default (r3 = 0, outputs untouched; the 40-line log cap hid them):
  cellFontRenderSurfaceInit 0x90B9465E: fills the guest CellFontRenderSurface (0x24 bytes: buffer, widthByte,
    pixelSizeByte, width, height, scissor x0 y0 x1 y1), default scissor = the whole surface.
  cellFontRenderSurfaceSetScissor 0xB422B005: stores the clip rect (x0, y0, x0+w, y0+h, clipped to the surface).
  cellFontRenderCharGlyphImageHorizontal 0x1A218FE4: a context handler (x, y arrive in f1/f2). Rasterises the glyph
    with stb_truetype at the scale the metrics functions use, origin on the baseline, clips it to the scissor, copies
    the coverage into a font-owned guest buffer (the cellFontOutline.c pool) and fills CellFontImageTransInfo (image,
    imageWidthByte, imageWidth, imageHeight, surface position, surfWidthByte) plus the glyph metrics, as the plain
    cellFontRenderCharGlyphImage does. Without a transInfo it writes the coverage into the surface.
  cellFontOpenFontInstance 0x29329541: a new handle sharing the opened font's data (and fontset chain), own scale.
  cellFontOpenFontsetOnMemory 0x073FA321: same as cellFontOpenFontset.
Also fixes cellFontOpenFontMemory / cellFontOpenFontFile, which took the CellFont as the first argument: the SDK
order is (library, data, size, subNum, uniqueId, font) / (library, path, subNum, uniqueId, font). They wrote the handle
into the library object, so every Toro font kept handle 0 and resolved to slot 0 by accident. subNum now selects the
face of a collection, OpenFontFile goes through cellfs_translate_path, CELL_FONT_MAX_OPENED is 64 (was 8; one
instance per font per text object), closing a font frees its image buffer and hands borrowed data over to an instance
still using it. Diagnostic: OpenFontMemory prints, once per font buffer, the stb parse result (valid/INVALID), the
first 4 bytes and the size.
Evidence (lifted EBOOT, work/recomp/ppu_recomp_045.cpp; layouts in the comment block at the end of cellFont.c):
0x14AA88 / 0x14AD30 SurfaceInit(obj+4, tex+0x80, px*w, px, w, h) with the object's own field at +0x28 (surface = 0x24
bytes), px = 4 (A8R8G8B8) or 2 (A4R4G4B4); 0x14A9C8 SetScissor(obj+4, x0, y0, w, h); 0x14BBE0 Horizontal(font, code,
surface, f1 x, f2 y, r8 metrics, r9 transInfo) with y = scissor y0 + baseLineY, advance read at metrics +0x10, '?'
redrawn when metrics width is 0; the title's transfer loops 0x14A338 / 0x14A410 read transInfo +0x00..+0x14 and
composite the coverage themselves; 0x14C3D4 / 0x14C4D4 open calls (font in r8 / r7); 0x14C5C4 OnMemory(lib, type,
font); 0x14C650 one OpenFontInstance(opened, new) per font per text object. Function semantics from the SDK names and
psdevwiki-level knowledge; RPCS3's cellFont.cpp read for behaviour only, nothing copied.
ASSUMPTION: scissor stored as x1/y1 exclusive (the title never reads it); without a transInfo the coverage byte fills
every byte of a pixel (max-blended); weight/slant effects are not applied.
Needs port/tlp/hle_tlp.cpp without its own cellFontInitializeWithRevision handler (removed 2026-10-19): that
handler returned 0 without initialising this library, so every cellFont call returned 0x80540003 (run tlp-7005: the
OpenFontsetOnMemory now implemented returned that error as the font index, OpenFontMemory was refused silently).
Also (run tlp-7007, firmware fonts present):
  Renderer API in SDK form: cellFontCreateRenderer(library, config, renderer), Bind(font, renderer),
    Destroy(renderer), the renderer being a title-owned object passed by address (0x14C6C4 r5 = obj+4, 0x14A898 r4 =
    obj+4, 0x14B8F4 r3 = obj+4). The old 2-argument Create with a table of 4 renderers failed the 5th text object with
    0x80540005 and every bind with 0x80540002.
  cellFontSetupRenderScalePixel no longer sets the font scale (KZ3 patch 5005 mapped it onto SetScalePixel). Toro calls
    it after every bind with 0.0, 0.0 (0x14A8A8, f1 = f2 = TOC-0x5248 = 0.0f), which made every font's scale 0: all
    metrics and layouts 0 and no glyph. ASSUMPTION: w, h > 0 still sets the scale (KZ3), 0 leaves it.
  Metrics and glyph bitmaps of a fontset come from the first file of its chain that has the code (they used only the
    first loadable file, SR-R-LATIN for 0x10B, so kana/kanji had no glyph); scale per face.
  Diagnostics: the first TLP_FONT_TRACE (default 16) calls of SetScalePixel, SetupRenderScalePixel,
    GetHorizontalLayout, GetRenderCharGlyphMetrics and RenderCharGlyphImageHorizontal (inputs, outputs, surface and
    transInfo) and of RenderSurfaceInit print one line each. TLP_FONT_BLANK=1 (default off) hands out no glyph image,
    metrics unchanged: a bisect switch for the image path.
  Scale: a pixel scale maps onto the face's OS/2 win cell (usWinAscent + usWinDescent; hhea ascent/descent when there
    is no OS/2), baseLineY = win ascent, lineHeight = cell + hhea lineGap, effectHeight = cell. stb's
    ScaleForPixelHeight (hhea) put capitals 6 px above the scissor Toro sets from baseLineY/effectHeight/lineHeight
    (run tlp-7008, "Ihe cat's name was Ioro"). ASSUMPTION, reasoning in font_face_metrics.
Switch: none (TLP_FONT_TRACE, TLP_FONT_BLANK diagnostics only).
Verify: build; run to the Story memory book: the log has one "[cellFont] OpenFontMemory(... stb=valid magic=...)" line
per font, no [hle] cellFont error lines, no "unresolved NID 0x1A218FE4 / 0x90B9465E / 0xB422B005 / 0x29329541 / 0x073FA321" (patch 7006), and the
speech bubble shows its text.

